## Ingredients

### Bara (Fried Flatbread)

- [ ] 2 cups [all-purpose flour]{ ing="all-purpose-flour" }
- [ ] 1/2 teaspoon [salt]{ ing="salt" }
- [ ] 1 teaspoon [instant yeast]{ ing="instant-yeast" }
- [ ] 1/2 teaspoon [baking powder]{ ing="baking-powder" }
- [ ] 2 teaspoons [sugar]{ ing="sugar" }
- [ ] 1/4 teaspoon [ground turmeric]{ ing="ground-turmeric" }
- [ ] 1/2 teaspoon [ground cumin]{ ing="ground-cumin" }
- [ ] 1 cup [lukewarm water]{ ing="lukewarm-water" } (adjust as needed)
- [ ] 1 tablespoon [vegetable oil]{ ing="vegetable-oil" } (for dough)
- [ ] [Vegetable oil]{ ing="vegetable-oil" } for frying

### Channa (Curried Chickpeas)

- [ ] 2 (14-15 oz) cans [chickpeas]{ ing="chickpeas" }, drained and rinsed
- [ ] 2 tablespoons [vegetable oil]{ ing="vegetable-oil" }
- [ ] 1 small [onion]{ ing="onion" }, finely chopped
- [ ] 3-4 [garlic cloves]{ ing="garlic-cloves" }, minced
- [ ] 1 tablespoon [curry powder]{ ing="curry-powder" }
- [ ] 1 teaspoon [ground cumin]{ ing="ground-cumin" }
- [ ] 1/2 teaspoon [ground turmeric]{ ing="ground-turmeric" }
- [ ] 1/4 teaspoon [amchar masala]{ ing="amchar-masala" } (optional, or use garam masala)
- [ ] 1-1.5 cups [water]{ ing="water" }
- [ ] 1 teaspoon [salt]{ ing="salt" } (adjust to taste)
- [ ] 1/2 teaspoon [black pepper]{ ing="black-pepper" }
- [ ] 2 tablespoons fresh [cilantro or culantro]{ ing="cilantro-or-culantro" }, chopped
- [ ] [Hot pepper sauce]{ ing="hot-pepper-sauce" } to taste (optional)

### Condiments (Optional)

- [ ] [Cucumber chutney]{ ing="cucumber-chutney" }
- [ ] [Tamarind sauce]{ ing="tamarind-sauce" }
- [ ] [Scotch bonnet pepper sauce]{ ing="scotch-bonnet-pepper-sauce" }
- [ ] [Mango chutney]{ ing="mango-chutney" }

## Recipe

### Make the Bara

1. In a large bowl, combine [flour]{ ing="all-purpose-flour" }, [salt]{ ing="salt" }, [yeast]{ ing="instant-yeast" }, [baking powder]{ ing="baking-powder" }, [sugar]{ ing="sugar" }, [turmeric]{ ing="ground-turmeric" }, and [cumin]{ ing="ground-cumin" }
1. Gradually add [lukewarm water]{ ing="lukewarm-water" } while mixing until a soft, slightly sticky dough forms (you may not need all the [water]{ ing="water" })
1. Knead for 5-8 minutes until smooth and elastic
1. Rub dough with 1 tablespoon [oil]{ ing="vegetable-oil" }, cover with a damp cloth, and let rise in a warm place for 1.5-2 hours until doubled in size
1. Punch down dough and divide into 16 equal pieces, rolling each into a ball
1. Cover balls with a damp cloth and let rest for 15-30 minutes

### Make the Channa

1. While dough is rising, heat 2 tablespoons oil in a large skillet or pot over medium heat
1. Add [onion]{ ing="onion" } and cook until softened, about 5 minutes
1. Add garlic and cook for 1 minute until fragrant
1. Stir in [curry powder]{ ing="curry-powder" }, cumin, turmeric, and [amchar masala]{ ing="amchar-masala" }; cook for 1-2 minutes to toast spices
1. Add [chickpeas]{ ing="chickpeas" } and stir to coat with spice mixture
1. Pour in 1 cup water, add salt and [black pepper]{ ing="black-pepper" }
1. Bring to a simmer, then reduce heat and cook for 20-25 minutes, stirring occasionally and adding more water if needed to maintain a thick, gravy-like consistency
1. Use the back of a spoon to lightly mash some chickpeas for a creamier texture
1. Stir in fresh cilantro and [hot pepper sauce]{ ing="hot-pepper-sauce" } if using; adjust seasoning
1. Keep warm while frying bara

### Fry the Bara

1. Heat about 2 inches of vegetable oil in a deep pot or skillet to 350-375°F (medium-high heat)
1. On an oiled surface or between oiled hands, flatten each dough ball into a 5-6 inch round, about 1/4 inch thick
1. Carefully slide one round into the hot oil; it should puff up immediately
1. Fry for 15-30 seconds per side until golden brown and puffy
1. Remove with a slotted spoon and drain on paper towels
1. Stack fried bara to keep warm and allow them to steam slightly and soften

### Assemble

1. Place one bara on a plate, add 2-3 tablespoons of channa in the center
1. Add desired condiments ([cucumber chutney]{ ing="cucumber-chutney" }, [tamarind sauce]{ ing="tamarind-sauce" }, [pepper sauce]{ ing="scotch-bonnet-pepper-sauce" })
1. Top with a second bara, pressing gently to create a sandwich
1. Serve immediately while warm

## Notes

- For more authentic flavor, use 1/2 lb dried chickpeas: soak overnight with 1/2 teaspoon baking soda, drain, rinse, then boil in fresh water with 1/2 teaspoon baking soda for 1 hour until tender before proceeding with step 7
- Culantro (also called bandhaniya or shadow beni) is traditional but cilantro works well as a substitute
- Bara should be soft, pillowy, and flexible, not dense or crispy
- The dough can rise for up to 6 hours for even better flavor and texture
- Makes approximately 8 doubles (16 bara)
- This is Trinidad’s most popular street food, originating in Princes Town in 1936 when customers requested a second piece of bara to sandwich the channa filling, thus creating “doubles”
